{
  "image_name": "2b076ebbaa898dadb261f2f25b244d17.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* interacts with macrophages and where host-directed therapies might intervene. The bacterium is first engulfed by phagocytosis after recognition by pattern-recognition receptors, but instead of being efficiently destroyed it appears to block normal phagosome maturation and phagolysosome fusion, including through ManLAM. A lysosome is shown as the compartment that should fuse with the phagosome to promote killing, while an ESX-1–mediated permeabilization route seems to let bacteria access the cytosol.\n\nSeveral protective host pathways are highlighted. One strategy is to restore phagolysosome acidification and maturation, with metformin and imatinib noted as examples. Other routes include induction of autophagy and ubiquitin-associated handling of the bacterium, and boosting antimicrobial peptides such as cathelicidin, with vitamin D appearing in both categories.\n\nThe figure also emphasizes the inflammatory side of infection. IFNγ from a T cell acts through its receptor on the infected cell, and a broad inflammatory program is listed, including cytokines such as TNF, inflammasome activity, reactive oxygen species, matrix metalloproteinases, and lipid mediators. A parallel box lists anti-inflammatory or tissue-protective approaches, such as corticosteroids, TNF blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, statins, COX inhibitors, and MMP inhibitors.",
